//? Title: DPLL: Actual Propagation, Restored Backtracking and Finite Random-SAT Experiments
//? Subtitle: Two-variable proofs, the original model and 25-variable sweep, checked CNF inputs and an iterative search stack
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given a Boolean formula in conjunctive normal form, we want either a
true/false assignment satisfying every clause or a decision that no such
assignment exists. Enumerating all assignments can require exponentially
many cases. DPLL combines forced unit assignments, satisfiability-preserving
pure choices and true/false branching to reduce that search. We begin
with a two-variable implication and a complete three-node refutation,
then retain the original satisfiable formula, contradiction and random
25-variable experiment. Actual owned event traces expose propagation
reasons and restored parent states. Measured search work and sampling
intervals qualify the finite sweep; it is not a proof of an asymptotic
3-SAT threshold. The canonical core validates all CNF literals and uses
an explicit heap stack rather than recursion by decision depth.
)

\toc

> Begin with two forced assignments

Let C₀=(x₁) and C₁=(¬x₁∨x₂). A satisfying model must set x₁=true
because C₀ is a unit clause. C₁ then has only x₂ unassigned, forcing
x₂=true. No split is needed. The total returned model is [0,+1,+1];
index zero is deliberately unused. Event snapshots use zero for
unassigned variables, while the final SAT model uses only +1 or −1
at variable positions.

The program records actual states from the same kernel as the original
solver. Clause indices in unit/conflict events refer to the original
input. All other events use the clause-count sentinel, displayed as a
dash in this table.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dpll_units.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::dpll_sat_trace(2,{{1},{-1,2}});
  const std::array<const char*,7> names{"enter","unit","pure","conflict","backtrack","exhausted","satisfied"};
  std::print("| event | literal | original reason clause | partial assignment |\n| :--- | ---: | :--- | :--- |\n");
  for(const auto& e:t.events)
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",names[static_cast<int>(e.kind)],e.literal,e.clause<t.clauses.size()?std::to_string(e.clause):"—",e.assignment);
  std::print("\nSAT {}; total model {}; nodes {}; unit assignments {}; decisions {}; captured entries {}.\n",t.solve.result.satisfiable,t.solve.result.assignment,t.solve.work.nodes,t.solve.work.unit_steps,t.solve.work.decisions,t.captured_entries);
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(80,1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle box;box.width=3.8;box.height=1.1;box.font_size=23;
  d.node("first",{0,1.5},"C₀ = (x₁)\nforce x₁ = true",box)
   .node("second",{5.5,1.5},"C₁ = (¬x₁ ∨ x₂)\nforce x₂ = true",box)
   .node("model",{2.75,-1},std::format("SAT: model {}\n{} search node; no split",t.solve.result.assignment,t.solve.work.nodes),box)
   .edge("first","second","propagate")
   .edge("second","model")
   .text({2.75,2.8},"Unit clauses give necessary assignments",26)
   .save_svg("build/dpll_units.svg");
}
