\caption(The actual root trace forces x1 from clause zero and x2 from clause one, then satisfies both clauses without a branch.)
\alttext(A native implication diagram connects the unit x1 to the forced x2 and the returned total model, with one search node.)

> The search rules and their proof obligations

A CNF formula is an AND of clauses, each an OR of signed literals.
Literal +v means xᵥ, and −v means ¬xᵥ. If a clause contains one
unassigned literal and every other literal is false, satisfying the
formula requires that literal to be true. Repeating this rule yields
unit propagation.

A pure literal appears in only one polarity among currently unsatisfied
clauses. Choosing that polarity can satisfy those clauses without
breaking a clause already satisfied by another assigned variable.
It therefore preserves existence of a model. Unlike a unit assignment,
a pure choice is not necessarily logically forced. The example below
will exhibit another model with a different pure-variable value.

When neither rule applies, split on an unassigned variable v:

\[
\operatorname{SAT}(F\mid A)=
\operatorname{SAT}(F\mid A,v=\mathrm{true})\ \lor\
\operatorname{SAT}(F\mid A,v=\mathrm{false}).
\]

The two branches exhaust all possible values of v. A clause with every
literal false proves the current branch inconsistent. Both branches
failing proves their parent inconsistent. Each new assignment fixes a
previously unassigned variable, so decision depth is at most n. A
partial state already satisfying every clause can be completed by
setting all remaining don't-cares true, as the original API does.
Together these facts establish soundness, completeness and termination.
Worst-case search remains exponential; this implementation does not
include clause learning, watched literals, restarts or a timeout.

> A complete two-variable UNSAT tree

Take all four binary clauses:
C₀=(x₁∨x₂), C₁=(x₁∨¬x₂), C₂=(¬x₁∨x₂), C₃=(¬x₁∨¬x₂).
There is no initial unit or pure literal. The solver splits x₁ true
first. Clause C₂ forces x₂=true, falsifying C₃. It returns to the
unchanged parent assignment [0,0,0], then tries x₁=false. Clause C₀
forces x₂=true, falsifying C₁. Both branches fail, so the root is
exhausted. The root itself has no falsified clause under its empty
partial assignment; its justification is the completed split.

The actual event sequence makes these distinct failure reasons explicit.
The tree has one decision, two attempted branches, three entered nodes,
two conflicting leaves and two restored-parent backtracks.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dpll_refutation.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::dpll_sat_trace(2,{{1,2},{1,-2},{-1,2},{-1,-2}});
  const std::array<const char*,7> names{"enter","unit","pure","conflict","backtrack","exhausted","satisfied"};
  std::vector<dm::algorithms::DpllEvent> conflicts;
  std::print("| event | node | depth | literal | reason clause | partial assignment |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- | :--- |\n");
  for(const auto& e:t.events) {
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",names[static_cast<int>(e.kind)],e.node,e.depth,e.literal,e.clause<t.clauses.size()?std::to_string(e.clause):"—",e.assignment);
    if(e.kind==dm::algorithms::DpllEventKind::conflict) conflicts.push_back(e);
  }
  const auto& w=t.solve.work;
  std::print("\nSAT {}; nodes {}; decisions {}; branches {}; conflicts {}; backtracks {}; captured entries {}.\n",t.solve.result.satisfiable,w.nodes,w.decisions,w.branches,w.conflicts,w.backtracks,t.captured_entries);
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(80,1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle box;box.width=4.8;box.height=1.2;box.font_size=21;
  d.node("root",{0,2},"node 0: split x₁\nparent state [0,0,0]",box);
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<conflicts.size();++i) {
    const auto& e=conflicts[i];const std::string key=std::to_string(e.node);
    d.node(key,{i?3.4:-3.4,-.7},std::format("node {}: x₁={}, x₂={}\nconflict in C{}",e.node,e.assignment[1]>0,e.assignment[2]>0,e.clause),box)
     .edge("root",key,i?"try false":"try true");
  }
  d.text({0,3.4},"Both actual branches fail",27)
   .text({0,-2.1},"Restore the parent after each failure; exhaust the root → UNSAT",21)
   .save_svg("build/dpll_refutation.svg");
}
```
