\alttext(A three-node native tree splits x1, forces x2 true in each child and marks the two conflicting original clauses, then declares the root exhausted.)

> Retain the original model and distinguish a pure choice from a forced value

The original formula is
(x₁∨x₂)∧(¬x₁∨x₃)∧(¬x₂∨¬x₃)∧(x₁∨¬x₃).
Its deterministic returned model remains x₁=true,x₂=false,x₃=true.
The original direct contradiction (x₁)∧(¬x₁) remains UNSAT.
The total-model checker below validates the original clauses rather
than trusting the solver's verdict alone.

For a separate tiny pure-literal example, use
G=(x₁∨x₂)∧(¬x₁∨x₃). Variable x₂ initially appears only positively,
so the solver chooses x₂=true. The remaining clause permits the pure
choice x₁=false, after which x₃ is a don't-care filled true. But
x₁=true,x₂=false,x₃=true also satisfies G. Thus the initial pure choice
preserves satisfiability without being entailed by G.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dpll_models.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::vector<std::vector<int>> original{{1,2},{-1,3},{-2,-3},{1,-3}};
  const auto s=dm::algorithms::dpll_sat_trace(3,original);
  const auto u=dm::algorithms::dpll_sat(1,{{1},{-1}});
  std::print("Original formula: SAT {}; model {}; model check {}.\n",s.solve.result.satisfiable,s.solve.result.assignment,dm::algorithms::cnf_assignment_satisfies(3,original,s.solve.result.assignment));
  std::print("Original contradiction: SAT {}; model size {}.\n",u.satisfiable,u.assignment.size());
  const std::vector<std::vector<int>> g{{1,2},{-1,3}};
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::dpll_sat_trace(3,g);
  const std::vector<int> other{0,1,-1,1};
  std::print("\n| pure assignment | partial state after choice |\n| ---: | :--- |\n");
  for(const auto& e:t.events) if(e.kind==dm::algorithms::DpllEventKind::pure)
    std::print("| {} | {} |\n",e.literal,e.assignment);
  const bool solver_ok=dm::algorithms::cnf_assignment_satisfies(3,g,t.solve.result.assignment);
  const bool other_ok=dm::algorithms::cnf_assignment_satisfies(3,g,other);
  std::print("\nPure-choice model {}; check {}. Alternative model {}; check {}.\n",t.solve.result.assignment,solver_ok,other,other_ok);
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(78,1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle box;box.width=4.8;box.height=1.2;box.font_size=23;
  d.node("formula",{0,2},"G = (x₁ ∨ x₂) ∧ (¬x₁ ∨ x₃)\nx₂ appears only positively",box)
   .node("solver",{-3.4,-.6},"solver: (false,true,true)\nx₂ chosen true",box)
   .node("other",{3.4,-.6},"also valid: (true,false,true)\nx₂ is false",box)
   .edge("formula","solver","pure choice").edge("formula","other","another model")
   .text({0,3.35},"A pure choice is safe, but need not be forced",26)
   .save_svg("build/dpll_models.svg");
}
