> Repeat the original finite random 3-SAT sweep and measure search work

Retain n=25 variables, seed 4242, 120 trials per ratio and all 21 requested
ratios from 2 to 7 in quarter steps. The canonical generator preserves
the original mt19937 stream: draw an id by rng()%n, reject repeated
variables within a clause before drawing its sign, and allow repeated
clauses. This has a tiny modulo bias, so it is a reproducible legacy
sampling scheme rather than an exactly uniform mathematical sampler.
The stream is continuous across trials and ratios; it is not reset.

The original code uses m=floor(requested_ratio·25). Some requested
quarter ratios therefore differ from the actual integer-clause ratio:
for example,2.25 gives56 clauses and actual m/n=2.24. The table and
plots below use those exact counts. Every sampled SAT model is checked.
The workload panel records median entered nodes using the same solver
and instances, which makes its difficulty claim directly testable.

Wilson 95% intervals come from the existing proportion-test API and
summarize binomial sampling uncertainty under an independent-trial
interpretation. They are not confidence limits for an asymptotic
threshold. The historical4.26 number is not drawn as a proven boundary:
a single n=25 sample cannot establish a limiting threshold, convergence
with n, or a universal solver-hardness location. Measured medians also
depend on this first-variable, true-first, unit/pure implementation.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dpll_random.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::mt19937 engine(4242);
  std::vector<double> ratio,fraction,lower,upper,median_nodes;
  std::print("| requested ratio | clauses | actual m/n | SAT / 120 | fraction | Wilson low | Wilson high | median nodes |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(int quarter=8;quarter<=28;++quarter) {
    const int m=quarter*25/4;std::size_t sat=0;
    std::vector<double> nodes;
    for(int trial=0;trial<120;++trial) {
      const auto f=dm::algorithms::random_3sat_formula(25,m,engine);
      const auto r=dm::algorithms::dpll_sat_work(25,f);
      if(r.result.satisfiable) {
        if(!dm::algorithms::cnf_assignment_satisfies(25,f,r.result.assignment)) throw std::runtime_error("sample model invalid");
        ++sat;
      }
      nodes.push_back(r.work.nodes);
    }
    const auto ci=dm::stats::proportion_test_one_sample(sat,120);
    const double median=dm::median(nodes);
    ratio.push_back(m/25.0);fraction.push_back(sat/120.0);
    lower.push_back(ci.conf_int_lower);upper.push_back(ci.conf_int_upper);median_nodes.push_back(median);
    std::print("| {:.2f} | {} | {:.2f} | {} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.1f} |\n",quarter/4.0,m,m/25.0,sat,sat/120.0,ci.conf_int_lower,ci.conf_int_upper,median);
  }
  auto p=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  p.line(ratio,fraction,"SAT fraction",{124,58,237})
   .points(ratio,fraction,"",{124,58,237},5)
   .segments(ratio,lower,ratio,upper,{148,163,184},1.4)
   .title("Finite sample: n=25, 120 trials per point")
   .x_label("actual clauses / variables").y_label("observed SAT fraction; Wilson 95% bars").y_limits(-.02,1.22);
  auto q=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  q.line(ratio,median_nodes,"median nodes",{37,99,235}).points(ratio,median_nodes,"",{37,99,235},5)
   .title("Actual search work on these same samples")
   .x_label("actual clauses / variables").y_label("median entered search nodes")
   .y_limits(0,*std::max_element(median_nodes.begin(),median_nodes.end())*1.35+1);
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(p).add(q).size(1100,550)
   .title("Original random experiment: measured finite-size behavior, not a threshold proof")
   .fit_panels().save_svg("build/dpll_random.svg");
}
```
