\caption(All 2520 original-stream formulas are re-solved with checked SAT models. Observed SAT fractions, approximate Wilson intervals and median nodes describe these finite samples rather than proving a limiting threshold.)
\alttext(Two panels show observed SAT fractions with sampling intervals and median entered nodes across the original twenty-one integer-clause ratios.)

In this executed sample, the largest median is 54.5 entered nodes at actual
ratio 112/25=4.48, where 58 of 120 formulas are SAT. The curve later has
small nonzero fractions again: 2/120 at 156/25=6.24 and 1/120 at 7.00.
Each ratio samples fresh formulas, so finite observed fractions need
not decrease monotonically. Those counts are retained rather than
replaced by the old abbreviated prose's “remains zero” summary.

> Validate inputs, bound capture and expose a deliberately unreduced family

The canonical solver validates every literal before any verdict or dense
allocation, even after an empty clause or inside a tautology. The bound
must be nonnegative; literals must be nonzero signed ids within 1…n,
with INT_MIN rejected before absolute-value arithmetic. Empty CNF is
true, including n=0; an empty clause is false. Returned SAT models have
n+1 entries and fill don't-cares true; UNSAT returns an empty model.

Original clause/literal order, duplicate occurrences and tautologies
are retained to preserve deterministic behavior. A raw duplicate can
delay unit detection because it has more than one unassigned occurrence.
Presence bits avoid polarity-counter overflow. Search still finds an
exact result. Its explicit frame stack avoids native recursion depth,
but dense assignments cost n+1 entries per live frame and can require
O(n²) live model storage along a deep branch.

The trace's default cap is one million logical entries. Input costs
1+number_of_clauses+number_of_literals. Each event costs 1+(n+1) for
its record and complete partial assignment. The cap is checked before
the affected copy, and input/root capacity before allocating the initial
dense state. Zero disables only this optional cap. It is not a byte,
time or total working-memory quota. An exceeded cap throws without a
partial SAT/UNSAT verdict. The two-unit example has 22 entries, so 22
passes and 21 fails.

To make the effect of preserving raw clauses visible, construct n
clauses (xᵢ∨¬xᵢ). They are all tautologies, but this implementation does
not remove them. It enters a true-first chain until all variables are
assigned: n+1 nodes, depth n, no unit/pure assignments or conflicts.
At depth k, the status and unit scans each inspect2n−k literals; the
pure scan inspects4n−3k. Including the final satisfied scan gives

\[
\text{literal visits}=\frac{11n^2+7n}{2},\qquad
\text{clause visits}=3n^2+n.
\]

The following family verifies those exact counts. Linear node count
alone would obscure quadratic repeated scan work; neither plot is an
elapsed-time benchmark.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dpll_limits.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto exact=dm::algorithms::dpll_sat_trace(2,{{1},{-1,2}},22);
  int caps=0,invalid=0;
  try {(void)dm::algorithms::dpll_sat_trace(2,{{1},{-1,2}},21);} catch(const std::length_error&) {++caps;}
  try {(void)dm::algorithms::dpll_sat_trace(std::numeric_limits<int>::max(),{},1);} catch(const std::length_error&) {++caps;}
  for(const auto f:std::vector<std::vector<std::vector<int>>>{{{0}},{{3}},{{std::numeric_limits<int>::min()}},{{},{0}}}) {
    try {(void)dm::algorithms::dpll_sat(2,f);} catch(const std::invalid_argument&) {++invalid;}
  }
  try {(void)dm::algorithms::dpll_sat(-1,{});} catch(const std::invalid_argument&) {++invalid;}
  std::print("Exact capture entries {}; expected cap rejections {}/2; input rejections {}/5.\n",exact.captured_entries,caps,invalid);
  const auto zero=dm::algorithms::dpll_sat(0,{}),empty=dm::algorithms::dpll_sat(0,{{}});
  std::print("Zero-variable empty CNF: SAT {}, model {}. Empty clause: SAT {}, model {}.\n\n",zero.satisfiable,zero.assignment,empty.satisfiable,empty.assignment);
  std::vector<double> width,lit_ratio,clause_ratio;
  std::print("| variables | nodes | max depth | literal visits | derived visits | clause visits |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(int n:{16,32,64,128,256,512,1024}) {
    std::vector<std::vector<int>> f;
    for(int v=1;v<=n;++v) f.push_back({v,-v});
    const auto r=dm::algorithms::dpll_sat_work(n,f);const auto& w=r.work;
    const std::size_t size=n,expected=(11*size*size+7*size)/2;
    if(!r.result.satisfiable || w.literal_visits!=expected || w.clause_visits!=3*size*size+size || w.nodes!=size+1 || w.max_depth!=size || w.unit_steps || w.pure_steps || w.conflicts)
      throw std::runtime_error("tautology-family work mismatch");
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",n,w.nodes,w.max_depth,w.literal_visits,expected,w.clause_visits);
    width.push_back(std::log2(n));lit_ratio.push_back(w.literal_visits/(1.0*n*n));clause_ratio.push_back(w.clause_visits/(1.0*n*n));
  }
  auto p=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  p.line(width,lit_ratio,"literal visits",{37,99,235}).points(width,lit_ratio,"",{37,99,235},5)
   .line(width,clause_ratio,"clause visits",{220,38,38}).points(width,clause_ratio,"",{220,38,38},5)
   .title("Unreduced tautologies: measured scans / n²")
   .x_label("log2(number of variables)").y_label("visits divided by n²").y_limits(0,7)
   .size(1100,570).save_svg("build/dpll_limits.svg");
}
```
