\caption(Exact versus one-short capture limits and complete input rejection are executed before an iterative tautology-family sweep. Measured literal/clause scans agree with the derived quadratic formulas through depth1024.)
\alttext(Two normalized scan curves approach5.5 literal visits and3 clause visits per n-squared over powers-of-two variable counts, while the printed table records actual node counts and depth.)

> Verification and limits

Native tests compare all 512 canonical two-variable clause sets and all
19,683 ordered three-clause formulas over the 27 canonical three-variable
clauses against exhaustive model enumeration. They also check 800 seeded
raw formulas with duplicates, tautologies, empty clauses and unused
variables. Actual event certificates validate entered child states,
unit reasons, pure polarity, falsified clauses, unchanged restored parents,
both-failed exhaustion and the final checked total model. Counter identities
include nodes=branches+1 and, on a complete UNSAT tree, nodes=2·decisions+1
and conflicting_leaves=decisions+1.

Additional tests cover invalid ids/counts, model shape and totality,
exact/one-short/disabled capture limits, validation priority, owned copies,
the unchanged generator's draw order/state and a 1024-decision iterative
chain. Ordinary, work-only and traced results agree. Separate unchanged-
source probes reproduce an invalid-literal bounds failure and signed
num_vars+1 overflow in the old implementation; negative counts were also
accepted rather than rejected.

This is a transparent deterministic DPLL solver for propositional CNF.
A SAT result supplies a directly checkable model. UNSAT follows from the
completed search; the event sequence is not a standardized DRAT/LRAT
certificate. The random sweep measures this solver on this small sampling
scheme and cannot establish a general threshold or solver-independent
hardness claim.

> References

M. Davis, G. Logemann and D. Loveland (1962),
[A machine program for theorem-proving](https://doi.org/10.1145/368273.368557),
Communications of the ACM 5(7),394–397, introduces the backtracking
procedure. D. Mitchell, B. Selman and H. Levesque (1992),
[Hard and Easy Distributions of SAT Problems](https://cdn.aaai.org/AAAI/1992/AAAI 92-071.pdf),
AAAI 92,459–465, studies finite random-SAT distributions and search work.
The raw-occurrence ordering, pure choices, heap frames, event accounting,
validation and legacy modulo-based generator here describe the executed
datamunge variant.

